INT. FLORIST'S COLD ROOM - NIGHT

A walk-in refrigerator, ranks of white buckets, a hundred roses breathing out their sweetness. Frost on the shelves. A single bulb in a wire cage, humming.

DETECTIVE HARLOW QUINN, 40s, kneels on the tile in a camel coat, breath fogging. A black glove is wrapped around one finger of a dead man's hand, which has been laid on a bucket of lilies like an offering. She sets a small pen torch between her teeth and parts the lilies.

Her cell phone rings, shrill in the cold. She flinches, spits the torch into her palm, and answers.

QUINN
(low)
Quinn.

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
(over the phone, brisk)
Where are you?

QUINN
Cold room, Marlow's Flowers.

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
Alone?

QUINN
Yes.

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
Get out. Now. She's on her way.

QUINN
Who's on her way?

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
The florist. She's got a key. She's been on the phone with me for a minute, she's furious, she wants you off her property.

QUINN
Captain, the body's in her fridge.

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
Then you've got two minutes, Harlow.

Quinn snaps the torch back into her teeth. A latch clicks somewhere beyond the door. She goes still. The bulb hums. A shadow slides across the frosted glass.

QUINN
(muttering around the torch)
She's already here.

The handle turns. The door opens a crack.

Quinn doesn't move. A rose petal drifts down onto the dead man's hand.

CAPTAIN WREN (V.O.)
Harlow? Talk to me.

The door swings wide. Light spills in. Quinn lifts the phone to her ear and does not look up.

QUINN
She's here.
